     "text": "The patient's symptoms are consistent with a viral upper respiratory infection.\n\n**P:** \n- Continue symptomatic treatment with rest, hydration, and over-the-counter antipyretics as needed. \n- RTC if symptoms worsen or persist beyond 10 days.\n\n---\n\n**03/26/2023 Office Visit**\n\n**S:** The patient presents today for a follow-up on her aortic valve disease. She reports no new symptoms related to her heart condition. No chest pain, shortness of breath, or palpitations noted.\n\n**O:** \n- **Vitals:** BP 119/71, HR 70, RR 16, Temp 98.6°F\n- **Weight:** 122 lb\n- **Physical Exam:** \n - **HEENT:** Normocephalic, atraumatic. Pupils equal, round, reactive to light. Extraocular movements intact. \n - **Neck:** Supple, no lymphadenopathy. \n - **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. \n - **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally, no wheezing, rales, or rhonchi. \n - **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly. \n - **Extremities:** No edema, cyanosis, or clubbing.\n\n**A:** \n- Aortic valve disease - stable. The patient’s aortic valve disease remains stable with no new symptoms or changes in clinical status.\n\n**P:** \n- Continue current management and monitoring. \n- RTC in 3-6 months for follow-up evaluation.\n\n---\n\n**Note:** The patient has been noted to be non-compliant with her medications and has missed follow-up appointments on two occasions. \n\n**Social History:** The patient reports consuming approximately 12 alcoholic drinks per week. \n\n**Family History:** Significant for myocardial infarction in the father at age 65.\n\n**Page 10 of 10**\n"
